In article <51c1mr$58s@pt9201.ped.pto.ford.com>,
Bryan Miller <millerb@ACM.org> wrote:
> I have a server that listens to a well know port and forks
> off children upon request.  Since the server cannot wait
> for the children to die before going back to the port to
> listen, it produces defunct (zombie) processes.
>
> Isn't there a way to start the server with SIGCLD set
> to ignore?

If you really don't want to be notified with SIGCLD, you can periodically
call waitpid() to "reap" the exited children.

Something like:

	while ((pid = waitpid(-1, &status, WNOHANG)) != -1) {
	    /* do stuff for cleaning up dead children */
	}

This should clean up all the Zombies and not hang if one or more child
processes are still active.  This should work for Digital UNIX, Sun
and HP-UX.

Andy St.Martin
